    kind: work_item  # 4ab6
    title: "hypergumbo cache grows without bound (5.3 GB / 27 entries for one repo in one day) — nothing evicts; implement LRU eviction ON by default at a 5 GB cap, opt-out and tunable, with home-directory deletion safety"  # 4ab6
    status: todo_hard  # 4ab6
    priority: 1  # 4ab6
    parent:  # 4ab6
    tags:  # 4ab6
    - developer_experience  # 4ab6
    - cache  # 4ab6
    - safety_zones  # 4ab6
    isbefore: []  # 4ab6
    duplicate_of: []  # 4ab6
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 4ab6
    pr_ref:  # 4ab6
    description: "MEASURED 2026-08-17 on the development box, after a normal day of work:\n\n  $ hypergumbo cache-status --per-repo\n  Cache directory: ~/.cache/hypergumbo\n  Total size: 5.5 GB\n  By repo:\n    f36c63310c791188   5.3 GB   27 entries   (last used: today)\n    ...10 other repos, 120.4 MB and below\n\n27 entries for ONE repository, every one created the same day, ~196 MB each.\n\nMECHANISM: the cache key includes a WHOLE-TREE state_hash, so any edit anywhere in\nthe repo misses and mints a fresh ~200 MB entry. On an actively-developed repo --\nand on this one the tracker writes .ops files continuously -- essentially every\ninvocation is a miss. Growth is therefore roughly (runs/day x survey size), with no\nupper bound.\n\nNOTHING EVICTS. `keep_latest` appears in exactly one place, cli.py:3803, as an\nargument to the MANUAL `cache-clear --repo <id> --keep-latest N` subcommand. There\nis no automatic pruning on write, on startup, or on any schedule. The only\nautomatic behaviour is a WARNING: _DEFAULT_HONK_GB = 1.0 (cli.py:3561) prints a\n\"HG cache is N GB (threshold: 1.0 GB)\" notice with an inspect/prune/configure\nhint, tunable via HYPERGUMBO_CACHE_HONK_GB (0 silences).\n\nSO THE CURRENT DESIGN DISCLOSES THE PROBLEM AND THEN HANDS THE USER A CHORE. That\nis materially better than silent growth, and the honk is why this was noticed at\nall -- but a local-first CLI that accumulates GB/day of derived data the user did\nnot ask for, and only ever asks them to clean it up by hand, is not a defensible\ndefault. We were 5.5x past our own stated threshold with no action taken.\n\nOWNER DECISION (2026-08-17): implement eviction, ON BY DEFAULT, with a reasonable\ndefault cap around 5 GB, opt-out and size-tunable.\n\nDESIGN NOTES for whoever implements:\n  - The existing honk threshold (1.0 GB, HYPERGUMBO_CACHE_HONK_GB) and the new\n    eviction cap are DIFFERENT KNOBS with different jobs: one says \"you may want to\n    look at this\", the other says \"I reclaimed this\". Do not silently repurpose the\n    honk variable as the cap -- an existing user who set HYPERGUMBO_CACHE_HONK_GB=0\n    to silence a NOTICE must not thereby disable EVICTION, or we would turn a\n    cosmetic preference into data deletion.\n  - Eviction deletes user-visible artifacts, so it must go through the\n    safety_zones cache-delete wrapper (safety_zones.py:170), not a raw rmtree --\n    the `cache-clear could delete arbitrary paths` hole named in the Unreleased\n    changelog is the reason that wrapper exists.\n  - Prefer whole-entry, least-recently-used eviction at the (repo, state_hash)\n    granularity, and always keep at least the most recent entry per repo so a\n    warm re-run is never destroyed by a cold one elsewhere.\n  - Announce what was reclaimed. A cache that quietly deletes is a cache that gets\n    blamed for the next unrelated cold run.\n\nDELETION SAFETY -- OWNER CAVEAT (2026-08-17): \"eviction means removing files from\na location typically in the user's HOME directory. Not something to be done\nlightly. But also not something to NOT do, given how much disk use is involved.\"\nBoth halves are binding. The following are requirements, not preferences:\n\n  1. THE ENABLING INVARIANT, and it must be stated and defended: every byte under\n     the cache root is TOOL-GENERATED AND REGENERABLE. That is the only reason\n     automatic deletion is admissible at all. If anything non-derived is ever\n     written there, eviction silently becomes data loss -- so the eviction path\n     must delete only whole entries matching the cache's own\n     (repo_fingerprint)/(state_hash)/(analyzer_identity) layout, and must skip\n     anything under the root that does not match it rather than assuming it is\n     ours to remove.\n  2. Route every delete through the safety_zones cache-delete wrapper\n     (safety_zones.py:170). Never a raw shutil.rmtree on a composed path. The\n     \"cache-clear could delete arbitrary paths\" hole closed in the Unreleased\n     cycle is precedent for exactly this failure.\n  3. Resolve the root and REFUSE if it lands outside the expected user_cache zone\n     (symlinked or relocated XDG_CACHE_HOME, HOME unset, root == \"/\" or == $HOME).\n     Do not follow symlinks out of the zone during the walk.\n  4. Never delete the most recent entry per repo, even when a single repo exceeds\n     the whole cap -- evicting the warm entry to satisfy a byte budget makes the\n     next run slower for no benefit and looks like corruption.\n  5. Concurrency: another hypergumbo process may be READING an entry. Do not\n     delete an entry whose files are open / recently touched; a grace period on\n     last-used plus the existing per-entry locking discipline, not a bare mtime\n     sort.\n  6. Crash safety: rename-then-remove so an interrupted eviction cannot leave a\n     partially-deleted entry that still parses as a cache hit.\n  7. DISCLOSE. Print what was reclaimed and from where, at normal verbosity, on\n     the run that does it. A cache that deletes quietly gets blamed for the next\n     unrelated cold run, and the user has no way to reconstruct what happened.\n  8. Ship a dry-run so a user can see the eviction set before trusting it, and\n     honour the opt-out unconditionally.\n\n  KNOBS (final): default cap 5 GB, eviction ON. HYPERGUMBO_CACHE_MAX_GB tunes it;\n  0 disables eviction entirely. Kept SEPARATE from HYPERGUMBO_CACHE_HONK_GB per\n  the note above -- silencing a notice must never disable deletion, and disabling\n  deletion must never silence the notice."  # 4ab6
    fields: {}  # 4ab6
